    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Lawrence Butler** (72.05) and **Taylor Ward** (70.17), who benefit from the most favorable matchups on the board. Facing off against Troy Melton and Colin Rea respectively, both benefit from a +16% park adjustment and high efficiency against right-handed pitching. **Dylan Beavers** (68.38) similarly thrives in this high-ceiling environment, maintaining strong value despite an \"Elite Hold\" warning\u2014his speed remains a premium asset for LawDog in the stolen base category. While **Cal Raleigh** (66.37) faces a challenging pitcher in Phillips, his superior SP skill adjustment compensates for the park penalty, solidifying his role as our primary backstop.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **JJ Bleday** (53.51), who sits just outside the starting rotation. While **Bleday** offers consistent daily output, his ceiling was suppressed by the necessity of prioritizing speed-oriented profiles like **Tyler Freeman** (59.19) and **Zack Gelof** (58.18) to chase stolen base upside. We are keeping a close eye on **Yainer Diaz** (49.80) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (60.79), as both are awaiting official lineup confirmation. If **Diaz** is moved out of the #7 hole, we expect a marginal efficiency bump; conversely, should **Perdomo** fall in the order, his utility score could slide toward the bench threshold.\n\nRisk management remains a priority given the presence of high-end arms like Gerrit Cole. While **Jonathan Aranda** (58.16) is a start against Cole, we are monitoring the matchup closely as the SP skill drag is substantial. Meanwhile, **Bryce Harper** (54.54) remains in the UTIL slot despite a tough draw against a high-velocity arm; his elite xwOBA provides enough of a floor to warrant inclusion over the inconsistent bench options like **Masyn Winn** (37.56) or **Drew Gilbert** (34.94), who both fell well below our 40-point efficiency floor.",
